$$F(k)=\max\{M\ge0:\ \exists\ \text{Sidon } S\subseteq\mathbb N_0,\ |S|=k,\ \{0,1,\dots,M\}\subseteq 3S\}.$$
(Elements of $S$ larger than $M$ are useless, so $F$ is non-decreasing; small values: $F(3)=7$ via $\{0,1,3\}$, $F(4)=14$ via $\{0,1,4,6\}$, $F(5)\ge22$ via $\{0,1,3,7,12\}$.) Trivially $k^2-O(k^{3/2})\le F(k)\le\binom{k+2}{3}-1$.

Problem. Determine the order of growth of $F(k)$. In particular: does $\lim_{k\to\infty}\log F(k)/\log k$ exist, and is it $2$? Equivalently, is there $c>0$ with $F(k)\ge k^{2+c}$ for all large $k$, i.e. can a Sidon set that is a basis of order $3$ for an initial segment be thinner than $\sqrt{x}$? Also determine $F(k)$ exactly for small $k$, e.g. $k\le 12$.","grounding":"This is the finite, quantitative version of "Result: Sidon sets that are asymptotic bases of order 3". Pilatte shows that an infinite Sidon set $S$ with $3S\supseteq[n_0,\infty)$ exists; restricting to $S\cap[0,N]$ gives a $k$-element Sidon set covering $[n_0,N]$, but the construction says nothing about (i) covering a full initial segment $\{0,\dots,M\}$ with only $k$ elements, nor (ii) the trade-off between $k$ and $M$, i.e. whether the counting function of a Sidon basis of order 3 can be as small as $x^{1/(2+c)}$ (the counting-function lower bound $x^{1/3}$ is the only obstruction known in the direction of the trivial upper bound $F(k)\le\binom{k+2}{3}$). The problem also sits between the "economical bases" of "Result: An explicit economical additive basis" (few representations for order 2) and the Sidon condition (at most one representation for order 2), and is a Sidon analogue of the classical postage-stamp function $n(3,k)$, whose optimal sets are far from Sidon.","why_hard":"(1) Transferring Pilatte's infinite construction: it relies on Sawin's $\mathbb F_q[t]$ Montgomery-type result and produces a set whose small elements are uncontrolled, so initial segments are not covered and the counting function is essentially $\sqrt{x}$-type, giving no information on exponents above $2$. (2) Probabilistic constructions (random Sidon-like sets of density $x^{-1/2-\delta}$ with alterations): the Sidon requirement forces deletion of elements, and the standard deletion arguments only work when $3S$ has representation counts $\gg\log$, which already requires density close to $x^{-1/2}$, giving only $F(k)\gg k^2$ up to logs. (3) Algebraic Sidon sets (Singer/Bose/Erdős–Turán) are spread uniformly in $[0,k^2]$, so only about $\pi/6$ of their $\binom{k+2}{3}$ triple sums fall below $k^2$ and collisions among sums make covering $[0,k^2]$ fail; there is no known algebraic family with the strongly non-uniform spread needed. (4) Upper bounds: counting triple sums only gives $k^3/6$; improving it requires showing a Sidon set cannot have $3S$ nearly collision-free on a long initial segment, a $B_3$-type lower-bound problem for which current tools (e.g. Fourier/energy arguments) lose polynomial factors.","why_important":"A proof that $F(k)\ge k^{2+c}$ would give Sidon sets that are bases of order $3$ with counting function $x^{1/(2+c)}$, sharpening Pilatte's theorem and moving toward the natural barrier $x^{1/3}$ asked about by Erdős, Sárközy and Sós. A proof that $F(k)=O(k^{2})$ (or $k^{2+o(1)}$) would show that the Sidon condition is incompatible with thin bases of order $3$ on initial segments, a new structural restriction linking $B_2$ sets to additive bases. Exact small values and extremal sets would provide data for the Sidon analogue of the postage stamp problem.","resembles_known":"Postage stamp problem (restricted $h$-range $n(h,k)$) and Erdős–Sárközy–Sós question on Sidon sets as bases of order 3 (Erdős #157)","novelty_confidence":0.7,"finite_form":"Find a Sidon set $S\subseteq\mathbb N_0$ with $|S|=12$ such that $\{0,1,\dots,115\}\subseteq 3S$, i.e. every integer $0\le n\le 115$ is $a+b+c$ with $a,b,c\in S$ (repetitions allowed). (Equivalently, certify $F(12)\ge115$. Necessarily $0,1\in S$ and all useful elements are $\le115$; a greedy extension of $\{0,1,3,7,12,22,30\}$ reaches only about $F(7)\ge38$, and the number of 12-element Sidon subsets of $[0,115]$ is far too large for exhaustive search.)","candidate_format":"A JSON array of exactly 12 distinct non-negative integers, each at most 115, e.g. [0, 1, 3, 7, 12, 22, 30, 45, 60, 78, 95, 110].","checker_code":"def ch